.TH veldiff "" "Command"
.SH NAME
veldiff \- what changed between two revisions of a drawing
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBveldiff\fR \fIold.d new.d\fR
.br
\fBveldiff \-mark\fR \fIold.d new.d\fR > \fImarkup.d\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
veldiff -- what changed between two revisions of a drawing
Usage:	veldiff old.d new.d		the change list
	veldiff -mark old.d new.d	... as a MARKED-UP DRAWING
	Matching is by IDENTITY, not by line.  The EXIT STATUS
	is the change count, so make(1) gates a print on it.
.HE
.B veldiff
compares two revisions of a drawing made with
.BR vellum .
.PP
Matching is by IDENTITY, not by line.
.BR diff (1)
on two
.B .d
files drowns in reordering, because the line order is the Z-ORDER and
not the content.
Here symbols match by DESIGNATOR first \- R7 is R7 wherever it moved in
the list \- and everything else by exact (type, geometry, value)
equality.
There is no fuzzy matching and no tolerance: an object that moved one
unit is a remove plus an add, and on a grid that is the honest answer.
An object still standing exactly where it stood, saying something
else, reports as CHANGED.
.PP
One line each, in the
.B velcheck
shape:
.DS
.ta 8n
	new.d: R7 moved 40,20 -> 40,28
	new.d: R9 added (R 47k)
	new.d: C2 removed (C 100n)
	new.d: T "Supply, sheet 1" changed
.DE
The EXIT STATUS is the change count (capped at 254), so a Makefile
gates a release print on ``no drift since the approved revision''
exactly the way it gates on
.BR velcheck .
A change of sheet UNITS is reported rather than refused.
.SH "THE MARKUP"
.B \-mark
prints the answer AS A DRAWING on standard output: everything
unchanged on its own layers, ADDED objects bold, REMOVED objects
dashed on the annotation layer.
There is no new viewer and no new user interface, because the markup
is ORDINARY OBJECTS \-
.B velpic
draws it,
.B velplot
prints it, and the editor opens it.
.SH REVISIONS
A revision is a FILE, and the record is the directory: no SCCS, no
hidden state, no format change.
When the frame stamp's
.B $R
says B,
.B "cp amp1.d amp1.d.B"
is the whole ceremony.
.PP
The /usr/vellum/etc/project.mk skeleton turns that into a workflow:
.B rev:
copies every sheet to its
.BI . REV
twin,
.B drift:
runs this program on each sheet against that twin so its exit status
gates
.BR print: ,
and
.B review:
hangs the marked-up drawing on the wall instead.
The gallery ships the pair: /usr/vellum/eg/amp.d.A beside amp.d.
.SH EXAMPLES
.DS
.ta 8n
	veldiff amp.d.A amp.d
	veldiff -mark amp.d.A amp.d > amp.mark
	velplot -T ps -fit amp.mark | lpr
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B vellum ,
.B velcheck ,
.B velplot ,
.B velpic ,
.B make
.SH NOTES
A
.B \-mark
markup holds the new revision PLUS the removals, so a markup of two
crowded sheets can carry more than the 400 objects a drawing holds
when it is opened again; it always prints.
.PP
This is the one tool that holds two drawings at once, which is why it
is a tool of its own: nothing else pays for the second object table.
